{"title":"45-45-90 triangle","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><dl><dd><i>The title of this article has been romanized due to technical restrictions. The correct title should be <b><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/e/a/fea0e7960f17abf1f6c9233db700688daa60bba6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$45^{\\circ}-45^{\\circ}-90^{\\circ}$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"120\" height=\"13\" /> triangle</b>.</i></dd></dl>\n<p>A <b>45-45-90 triangle</b> is a triangle with 2 angles measuring <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/2/1/821d3f11ab7a3e93c7a404a34224228f0449505a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$45^{\\circ}$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"24\" height=\"13\" /> and one angle measuring <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/a/7/6a7dfa8a2a90d521b2b03f5b82a1fb9f85dce88c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$90^{\\circ}$\" width=\"24\" height=\"13\" />. It is a right isosceles triangle. The legs of any 45-45-90 triangle are congruent while the hypotenuse is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/1/4/114e91200cd4c857d233c7fed6cbe7451f3a82fc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sqrt2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"24\" height=\"18\" /> times the length of the legs. It is also important in geometry when the pythagorean theorem is involved. To find the area of a 45-45-90 triangle simply take the length of one of it's two congruent sides(the sides that are the legs, not the hypotenuse) and call it x, in which case the area is 0.5x^2.\n</p><p><br />\n<i>This article is a stub.  Help us out by <span class=\"plainlinks\"><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php?title=45-45-90_triangle&amp;action=edit\">expanding it</a></span>.</i>\n</p>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261018071530\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: []\nCPU time usage: 0.010 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.020 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 